  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="histoire-chapeau">
          AZERTY is one of the most-used objects in France, and one of the most poorly documented: its inventor is unknown, its most-discussed choices were never explained, and much of what you read about it is legend. Each question below gets a verdict and its sources; when the answer is “nobody knows”, it says so.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="histoire-legende">
        <span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span>
        <span class="verdict verdict--partiel">Partly documented</span>
        <span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span>
        <span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span>
      </div>
      <p class="histoire-date texte-petit texte-2">Published 22 July 2026</p>
    </div></div>
  </section>

    {# I. Origins #}
    <section class="section" id="origins">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>The origins</h2>

        <details class="notice" id="why-not-qwerty" open>
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span> Why does France use AZERTY instead of QWERTY?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The foundation is settled: AZERTY derives from the American QWERTY and appears in France in the 1890s, as a local adaptation of imported typewriters (<a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/AZERTY" hreflang="fr">French Wikipedia</a>). France did not invent its keyboard from scratch: it received an already-standardized American machine and modified it at the margins, with a few letters swapped, accents added and punctuation reshuffled.
            </p>
            <p>
              The rest is not settled. No period source (manufacturer catalogue, patent, commercial correspondence) explains why those particular letter swaps (A↔Q, Z↔W, the moved M). The explanations in circulation, such as making room for accents or improving French ergonomics, are retrospective reconstructions: plausible, never demonstrated. And they have a logical flaw: the letter swaps have nothing to do with the accents, which were added on other keys. You can explain why the French keyboard has accents; nobody has ever explained why it is called AZERTY.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="inventor">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span> Who invented the AZERTY keyboard, and when?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Nobody knows, and not for lack of trying. The contrast with QWERTY is striking: on the American side, an identified inventor (Christopher Latham Sholes), a dated patent (1878), a company (Remington) and a documented timeline. On the French side: no named inventor, no patent, no founding document. Contributors to <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/AZERTY" hreflang="fr">French Wikipedia</a> have documented, since 2009 on the article’s talk page, fruitless searches for primary sources.
            </p>
            <p>
              Only one chronological marker is solid. In 1907, the stenographer Albert Navarre proposed an alternative layout, the ZHJAY, designed from French letter frequencies. It failed commercially, and the reason is telling: professional typists were already trained on AZERTY and refused to relearn. In other words, by 1907 AZERTY was already entrenched enough to repel a better-thought-out rival. Its birth therefore sits somewhere in the last decade of the 19th century, without anyone being able to date it or attribute it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="origin-qwerty">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span> Where does QWERTY, which AZERTY descends from, come from?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The first mass-produced typewriter, developed by Christopher Latham Sholes, was sold by Remington from 1873. The traditional account, repeated everywhere for a century, credits the layout to a mechanical constraint: spacing out frequently struck letters so the type bars would not jam.
            </p>
            <p>
              A Japanese academic study (<a href="http://kanji.zinbun.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~yasuoka/publications/PreQWERTY.html">Yasuoka &amp; Yasuoka, “On the Prehistory of QWERTY”, 2011</a>) proposes a different genealogy, based on Sholes’s successive prototypes: the keyboard is said to have formed in the service of telegraph operators transcribing American Morse on the fly, a use in which certain ambiguous code letters had to sit close together to be corrected quickly. The layout then froze with the Teletype and the emerging typewriting industry.
            </p>
            <p>
              Who is right? Sholes never explained his choices, and both accounts coexist in the literature today. It is a lesson in humility that holds for this whole page: even the most documented keyboard in the world keeps its blind spots.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="slow-down-myth">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Was the QWERTY keyboard designed to slow typists down?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              <strong>No.</strong> It is the most widespread received idea in the whole history of the keyboard, and it is false. The decisive argument is chronological: fast ten-finger touch typing was invented <em>after</em> QWERTY was fixed. You cannot have set out to slow down typists who did not yet exist: in Sholes’s day, people typed with two or four fingers, looking at the keyboard.
            </p>
            <p>
              What the spacing of certain letter pairs targeted was the mechanical jamming of the type bars, not human speed: two neighboring bars struck in quick succession risked colliding. And even that goal was only partly pursued: E and R, one of the most frequent pairs in English, stayed side by side, which definitively wrecks the hypothesis of a systematic slowdown. The myth survives anyway, because it makes a better story than the truth.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="anti-anglo">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span> Was AZERTY created to set France apart from the Anglo-Saxons?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Nothing proves it. This so-called “cocorico” theory (after the French rooster’s crow, a byword for national pride), according to which France altered the American keyboard on principle, to assert its distinctiveness, circulates in the media in a light-hearted tone. But no period document supports it, and the articles that relay it generally begin by admitting that “nobody really knows why” before offering it as a fallback explanation. It is a seductive retrospective reconstruction; on the current state of the archives, a legend.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    {# II. The surprising choices #}
    <section class="section" id="surprising-choices">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>The surprising choices</h2>
        <p class="histoire-chapeau">
          These are the questions everyone asks and almost nobody answers. For good reason: their authors left no explanation. The keyboards themselves are often the only documents, and an AZERTY typewriter from <a href="https://typewriterdatabase.com/1951-hermes-baby.22759.typewriter">1951 (Hermes Baby)</a> serves here several times as a dated landmark.
        </p>

        <details class="notice" id="a-z-q-w">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span> Why are A and Q, and Z and W, swapped on the AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/AZERTY" hreflang="fr">French Wikipedia</a> puts it bluntly: “the reason for this change is unknown” (our translation). No attested French frequency study lies behind the choice, and no manufacturer catalogue explains it.
            </p>
