class="schema__touche" x="126" y="80" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="137.5" y="96.1" text-anchor="middle">B</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="153" y="80" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="164.5" y="96.1" text-anchor="middle">N</text><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="180" y="80" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre schema__lettre--marquee" x="191.5" y="96.1" text-anchor="middle">M</text><text class="schema__titre" x="0" y="135">AZERTY (France)</text><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="0" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre schema__lettre--marquee" x="11.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">A</text><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="27" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre schema__lettre--marquee" x="38.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">Z</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="54" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="65.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">E</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="81" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="92.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">R</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="108" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="119.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">T</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="135" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="146.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">Y</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="162" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="173.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">U</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="189" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="200.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">I</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="216" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="227.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">O</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="243" y="145" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="254.5" y="161.1" text-anchor="middle">P</text><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="9" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre schema__lettre--marquee" x="20.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">Q</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="36" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="47.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">S</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="63" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="74.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">D</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="90" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="101.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">F</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="117" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="128.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">G</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="144" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="155.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">H</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="171" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="182.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">J</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="198" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="209.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">K</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="225" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="236.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">L</text><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="252" y="172" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre schema__lettre--marquee" x="263.5" y="188.1" text-anchor="middle">M</text><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="18" y="199" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre schema__lettre--marquee" x="29.5" y="215.1" text-anchor="middle">W</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="45" y="199" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="56.5" y="215.1" text-anchor="middle">X</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="72" y="199" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="83.5" y="215.1" text-anchor="middle">C</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="99" y="199" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="110.5" y="215.1" text-anchor="middle">V</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="126" y="199" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="137.5" y="215.1" text-anchor="middle">B</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="153" y="199" width="23" height="23" rx="3"/><text class="schema__lettre" x="164.5" y="215.1" text-anchor="middle">N</text><text class="schema__note" x="0" y="266">A↔Q and Z↔W swapped;</text><text class="schema__note" x="0" y="286">M moved to the end of the middle row.</text></svg>
              <figcaption>Only three differences between the letters of QWERTY and AZERTY: A↔Q, Z↔W, and the moved M.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p>
              What can be said, though, is that the most commonly offered explanation, “the keyboard was adapted to French frequencies”, is refuted by the numbers. A is the <a href="https://fr.sttmedia.com/frequences-de-lettres-francais" hreflang="fr">second most frequent letter in French<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> (8.1% of characters); Q is the twenty-first (0.9%). Yet AZERTY did exactly the opposite of what those figures dictate: it drove A off the home row, where QWERTY placed it under the left little finger, to install Q there. The swap degrades typing comfort in French: whatever its explanation, it must be sought somewhere other than ergonomics.
            </p>
            <div class="histoire-hypothese">
              <p class="histoire-hypothese__label">Hypothesis: Antoine Olivier, co-founder of AZERTY Global</p>
              <p>
                What if the benefit sought was not the typing, but the name? The string “qwerty” has no grip on a French mouth; “azerty” reads like a word. My hypothesis is that the swaps on the left side of the keyboard may have served exactly that: giving the American machine a pronounceable top row, and therefore a nameable and sellable one on the French market. It has the merit of explaining what ergonomics cannot: a swap that degrades typing implies a benefit elsewhere.
              </p>
              <p>
                I had this hypothesis tested by an independent documentary search in July 2026: it could neither confirm nor refute it, and two elements call for caution. On one hand, the historians who combed the professional press of the era, such as <a href="https://www.persee.fr/doc/reso_0751-7971_1998_num_16_87_3163" hreflang="fr">Delphine Gardey<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>, describe sales arguments centered on the visibility of the typing, never on the name of a layout, and none mentions this angle. On the other hand, among our neighbors, when a variant’s motive is documented, it is frequency-based and not name-based: the German QWERTZ moved its Z for the digraphs “tz” and “zu”, not for its name.
              </p>
              <p>
                The absence of a competing explanation is not proof: I offer this hypothesis to the debate, no more. What would settle it: dating the first written attestation of the <em>word</em> “azerty” in the French press or catalogues. If it appears only long after the machines, the hypothesis falls.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="letter-m">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span> Why is the M next to the L on the AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              On QWERTY, the M sits bottom right, after the N; on AZERTY, it migrated to the far right of the middle row, after the L, leaving its original spot to the comma. This move is one of AZERTY’s most distinctive traits, since it is what most disorients QWERTY regulars, and its motive is documented nowhere.
            </p>
            <p>
              The explanations you meet online (balancing the load between the hands, bringing the comma closer to the space bar, clearing the bottom row) are internet speculation, with no period source. The only attested thing is the fact itself: the M to the right of the L appears on the oldest documented AZERTY machines and has never moved since.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="numbers-shift">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--partiel">Partly documented</span> Why do you have to press Shift to type numbers on an AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              This is the best-understood AZERTY choice, even if the initial decision is undated. The functional logic is clear: relegating the digits to the Shift position freed the direct top row for the everyday accented letters of French (é, è, ç, à), which English had no need for. A French text contains many more “é” than “4”: over a day of typing, the trade-off holds up.
            </p>
            <p>
              The compromise is attested at least since the typewriters of the 1950s (the <a href="https://typewriterdatabase.com/1951-hermes-baby.22759.typewriter">1951 Hermes Baby</a> already shows it), and it crossed into computing without ever being reconsidered. What is missing is the date and author of the initial decision, which no one has recovered. Note the paradox: the one AZERTY choice that explains itself well is also the one that today’s users, who type more digits and fewer letters than in 1900, contest the most.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="period-shift">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span> Why does the period need Shift on AZERTY, while the semicolon is directly accessible?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              This may be the strangest of all: the period, one of the most frequent characters in French, demands a combination, while the semicolon, one of the rarest, is directly accessible. The inconsistency is noted by every technical description of AZERTY; no historical source gives its reason, despite decades of user complaints.
            </p>
            <div class="histoire-hypothese">
              <p class="histoire-hypothese__label">Hypothesis: the capital that follows the period</p>
              <p>
                A hypothesis circulates among enthusiasts (notably in <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/france/comments/sytmcv/pourquoi_est_plus_accessible_que_sur_nos_claviers/" hreflang="fr">this r/france thread<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>), and it has the merit of thinking like a typist rather than a programmer. French grammar requires a capital after a period: on a typewriter, placing the period on the Shift row let a typist chain “period, then capital” without releasing and re-engaging the carriage between the two, a heavy gesture on mechanical machines, where the Shift key physically lifted the whole type basket. After a semicolon, by contrast, one continues in lowercase: direct access was the right register. Two period-specific uses reinforce it: telegrams, typed entirely in punctuated capitals, and French-style lists, each entry ending in a semicolon followed by lowercase.
              </p>
              <p>
                It is coherent, elegant, and unconfirmed: no period typing manual or manufacturer document has been found that spells out this reasoning. Verdict held: unknown.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="u-grave-key">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--inconnu">Unknown</span> Why is there a ù key on the AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The ù appears in practically a single French word, “où” (where), and yet has a dedicated key, attested at least since 1951. Measured against the keystrokes it serves, it is probably the least cost-effective key on the whole keyboard.
            </p>
            <p>
              The most common hypothesis is that it recycled a slot inherited from QWERTY, that of the American apostrophe, freed up during the French adaptation: rather than a decision in favor of ù, a simple filling of an empty slot. No source confirms it. A sign of the times: the 2019 AFNOR standard removed ù’s key precisely to reassign that slot to more useful characters.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="squared-key">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span> What is the ² key for, and where does it come from?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Contrary to a widespread idea, the ² key does not come from the typewriter: documented mechanical AZERTY models have none. It belongs to the computer era, which makes its existence even more curious, since someone, in the days of the first French PCs, had to decide to devote a whole key to the superscript two.
            </p>
            <p>
              A credible hypothesis links it to the French standardization work of the 1980s: Yves Neuville’s report (1985) envisaged a general superscript key, allowing ², ³ and others, of which only the ² would have survived the encoding constraints of PC keyboards. As the report is unfindable online, this lineage remains to be confirmed on the document itself. A detail that lends weight to a regional decision rather than an inheritance: Belgium, for its part, added a ³.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="dead-keys">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--partiel">Partly documented</span> Why do you need two keys to type ê, î or ü on an AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The <a href="https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Touche_morte" hreflang="fr">dead key<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> is a typewriter mechanism: a strike that prints the accent without advancing the carriage, followed by the letter that prints beneath it. The trade-off is arithmetic: a single circumflex dead key produces â, ê, î, ô, û, where direct access would demand five dedicated keys, on a machine that had only about forty.
            </p>
            <p>
              The split was made by frequency: five letters with diacritics (é, è, à, ù, ç) received a direct-access key, and everything else, circumflexes and diaereses, went to two strokes. The general principle is documented; the detail of the trade-offs (why ù and not ê, for instance) is not. Computing inherited the mechanism as is, down to the name: our software “dead keys” simulate a carriage that no longer exists.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    {# III. Gaps #}
    <section class="section" id="gaps">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>What AZERTY never learned to do</h2>

        <details class="notice" id="capitals-oe-quotes">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why can’t you type É, Ç, œ and « » on an AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Because AZERTY was never <em>designed</em>: it is a stack of one-off choices, inherited from the typewriter, never revised as a whole. Each era added its characters into the remaining slots, with no overall plan, and when the slots ran out, the missing characters simply stayed outside. Accented capitals, œ and French quotation marks are the most visible victims of that stacking.
            </p>
            <p>
              Computing could have set things right; it did the opposite. The Windows French keyboard driver (KBDFR.DLL) has never added É, Ç, œ or « » to direct access since its first version in 1993 (<a href="https://kbdlayout.info/KBDFR/">version history</a>): thirty years of Windows updates, zero French characters added. It is this finding, drawn up notably by <a href="https://www.afnor.org/decryptages/electrotechnologies/clavier-francais-norme-volontaire/" hreflang="fr">AFNOR<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> and the Ministry of Culture, that triggered the 2019 standardization, and it is the problem that modern alternative layouts also fix, including <a href="/en/comparison">AZERTY Global</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="programming">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why is the AZERTY keyboard poorly suited to programming?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              AZERTY was frozen at a time when braces, brackets and vertical bars did not exist as a writing need: in 1900, nobody typed <code>{ }</code>. When computing made them essential, they were tucked into the AltGr layers, the third function of the already-crowded number keys, with no overall reorganization. Hence three-finger combinations for characters that code uses constantly, where the American QWERTY serves them in one or two keystrokes.
            </p>
            <p>
              The 2019 standard committee actually listed programming among its optimization criteria: the institutional admission that it had never been taken into account. The frustration of French-speaking developers is widely reported, and many switch to QWERTY to code; its real cost, however, has never been measured by a study.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="zqsd">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why do French gamers use ZQSD instead of WASD?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              WASD took hold in the mid-1990s through the competitive <em>Quake</em> scene, popularized notably by the champion Dennis “Thresh” Fong, who used it to combine keyboard movement with mouse aiming, then became the industry standard when <em>Half-Life</em> adopted it as the default in 1998 (<a href="https://www.rtbf.be/article/pourquoi-utilise-t-on-zqsd-dans-tous-les-jeux-video-11405364" hreflang="fr">RTBF<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>).
            </p>
            <p>
              ZQSD is only its positional transposition: the same <em>physical</em> keys, read on an AZERTY keyboard where W and A are called Z and Q. Nobody “decided” this conversion: it happened mechanically, game after game, with no documented founding act, to the point of becoming a cultural marker of French-speaking gaming. It is also why the alternative layouts that move those four letters lose the gamers: their reflexes are wired to the positions, not the letters.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    {# IV. Variants #}
    <section class="section" id="variants">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>One AZERTY? No: many</h2>
        <p class="histoire-chapeau">
          “The” AZERTY does not exist: there is a family of layouts that share the letters and diverge on almost everything else, depending on the country, the operating system and the manufacturer. With no common standard for over a century, each settled things on its own.
        </p>

        <details class="notice" id="windows-mac-linux">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why is the Windows AZERTY different from the Mac and Linux one?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The physical keyboard is the same; the software driver is not. Each vendor developed its own independently, with no common reference: macOS and Linux (the “fr-oss” layout) have long exposed extra characters not engraved on the keys (accented capitals, œ, French quotation marks) which the historical Windows driver never added. The international standard ISO/IEC 9995 (1994) did lay down general principles for organizing keyboards, but never a common French layout that vendors were bound to follow.
            </p>
            <p>
              Concrete result: the same gesture does not produce the same character across systems. A Mac user types É without thinking (Caps Lock, then é); the same gesture under Windows writes a 2. This asymmetry, invisible as long as you stay on one machine, is one of the reasons so many French people believe that “É doesn’t exist on the keyboard”: it is true only on theirs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="belgian-keyboard">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> What is the difference between the Belgian and the French AZERTY?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The letters and the common accented characters are identical: a French person types on a Belgian keyboard without noticing, up to the first symbol. Because several symbols change place (§, !, *, £, µ, +, =), and the Belgian keyboard adds two things: a ³, and an acute-accent dead key, precious for Dutch (<em>vóór</em>), the other national language, and which incidentally gives access to É. The Belgian AZERTY is therefore not an imported French AZERTY: it is a bilingual adaptation.
            </p>
            <p>
              A notable point: no official Belgian keyboard standard has been identified. The Belgian variant is a de facto manufacturers’ standard, never formalized: the history of the French AZERTY, in miniature.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="quebec">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why doesn’t Quebec use the AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Because North American computing was built on QWERTY, and Canadian standardization followed its market rather than France. The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CSA_keyboard">CSA Z243.200</a> standard (1992) defines a QWERTY keyboard adapted to French: letters, digits and punctuation stay at the American positions, and French characters (é, è, à, ç) are added by direct access or dead keys.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Quebec case is valuable for understanding this whole history: it proves that you can write French correctly on a QWERTY base, and therefore that AZERTY is not a linguistic necessity. The language did not determine the keyboard; the continent did.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="francophonie">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why don’t all French-speaking countries use the AZERTY keyboard?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Because the keyboard follows the regional industrial market, never the language. France and Belgium inherited AZERTY from European typewriters; Quebec, the North American QWERTY standardized by the CSA; French-speaking Switzerland shares the Swiss national QWERTZ, a single keyboard designed to serve German, French and Italian speakers at once, whose precise institutional origin is, incidentally, not documented.
            </p>
            <p>
              Four regions, one language, three families of keyboards: no French-speaking body ever coordinated these choices, and none tried. Each community received the keyboard of its industrial environment, then adapted it to its language, never the other way round.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    {# V. Standardization #}
    <section class="section" id="standardization">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>Standardization: a century late</h2>

        <details class="notice" id="no-standard">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span> Why was the AZERTY keyboard never standardized before 2019?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Contrary to what is often read, there were attempts: two, both dead letters. In 1976, AFNOR published an experimental standard (NF XP E55-060), never taken to the definitive stage. In 1985, Yves Neuville’s report, commissioned by the administration, proposed to reorganize the keyboard’s symbols into logical blocks. Neither prevailed, for reasons the sources do not explain: one may suspect the inertia of the installed base and the absence of any obligation, but that is an inference, not a documented fact.
            </p>
            <p>
              AZERTY therefore remained a de facto standard for over a century: no text defined it, no body was responsible for it, and each manufacturer or system vendor made its own reading. It took until late 2015 for the Ministry of Culture, alarmed by the impossibility of writing French correctly on the keyboards sold in France, to approach AFNOR, the starting point of the process that would end in 2019.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="standard-2019">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> What is the new AZERTY, the 2019 “AZERTY standard”?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Published on 2 April 2019 by <a href="https://www.afnor.org/decryptages/electrotechnologies/clavier-francais-norme-volontaire/" hreflang="fr">AFNOR<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>, it defines two layouts: an improved AZERTY, with letters and digits unchanged and symbols entirely reorganized by statistical optimization, and a standardized version of bépo, the community ergonomic layout.
            </p>
            <p>
              Rare for an industrial standard: its design drew on human-computer interaction researchers (Aalto University, Inria), on real typing corpora and on optimization algorithms, rather than committee negotiation alone. A public consultation in 2017 gathered over 3,000 comments, unheard of for an AFNOR document. The whole method is documented in detail in <a href="https://inria.hal.science/hal-01826384" hreflang="fr">Nancel, 2018<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>, the methodological appendix published in open access: it is today the best single source on the recent history of the French keyboard.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="standard-adoption">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why didn’t the new standardized AZERTY replace the traditional AZERTY?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Because the standard is <em>voluntary</em>: it binds no one, not manufacturers, not system vendors, not administrations. Without a public-purchasing obligation or default pre-installation, a keyboard standard can rely only on the goodwill of the ecosystem; it found little.
            </p>
            <p>
              The figures speak: Windows only integrated the matching drivers (KBDFRNA.DLL and KBDFRNB.DLL, shipped with the 24H2 update) in October 2024, five and a half years after publication, and without making it the default layout. Conforming physical keyboards remain nowhere to be found in mainstream stores. The standard coexists with the traditional AZERTY; it has not replaced it, and nothing indicates it will.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    {# VI. Physical #}
    <section class="section" id="physical">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>The physical keyboard</h2>

        <details class="notice" id="staggered-rows">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why aren’t the keyboard’s keys aligned vertically?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              It is a mechanical fossil. On typewriters, each key drove a type bar, and all the bars had to find their own path to the striking point: the row stagger followed directly, since the keys of one row had to let the bars of the row below pass through (<a href="https://deskthority.net/wiki/QWERTY">Deskthority</a>).
            </p>
            <p>
              The constraint vanished with electronic keyboards; the stagger stayed, out of habit and compatibility with learned gestures. No study has shown any ergonomic benefit to keeping it: so-called ortholinear keyboards, which align the keys in a grid, remove it without the sky falling in. A hundred and fifty years after the invention of the type bars, and decades after they disappeared, our fingers still zigzag for them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="less-than-key">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--documente">Documented</span> Why do some AZERTY keyboards lack the &lt;&nbsp;&gt; key?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              The answer comes down to two acronyms: ISO and ANSI, the two great <em>physical</em> keyboard formats. The ISO format, standard in Europe, has 105 keys: an inverted-L Enter key over two rows, a short left Shift, and an extra key lodged between the left Shift and W, the &lt;&nbsp;&gt; key on AZERTY. The ANSI format, standard in the United States, has 104 keys: a horizontal Enter, a long left Shift, and no 105th key. The &lt; and &gt; characters live elsewhere there, on other keys of the American layout.
            </p>
