" x="225" y="111.5" text-anchor="middle">Shift</text><text class="schema__titre" x="0" y="164">ANSI (United States): 104 keys</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="0" y="176" width="44" height="29" rx="3"/><text class="schema__libelle" x="22" y="195.5" text-anchor="middle">Tab</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="47" y="176" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="79" y="176" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="0" y="208" width="52" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="55" y="208" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="87" y="208" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="0" y="240" width="70" height="29" rx="3"/><text class="schema__libelle" x="35" y="259.5" text-anchor="middle">Shift</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="73" y="240" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><text class="schema__libelle" x="87.5" y="259.5" text-anchor="middle">W</text><text class="schema__note" x="140" y="228" text-anchor="middle">…</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="168" y="176" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="200" y="176" width="80" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche" x="156" y="208" width="29" height="29" rx="3"/><rect class="schema__touche schema__touche--marquee" x="188" y="208" width="92" height="29" rx="3"/><text class="schema__libelle schema__lettre--marquee" x="234" y="227.5" text-anchor="middle">Enter</text><rect class="schema__touche" x="170" y="240" width="110" height="29" rx="3"/><text class="schema__libelle" x="225" y="259.5" text-anchor="middle">Shift</text><text class="schema__note" x="0" y="298">The 105th key (&lt; &gt;) exists</text><text class="schema__note" x="0" y="318">only in the ISO format.</text></svg>
              <figcaption>The ISO format (top) has a 105th key between the left Shift and W, absent from the ANSI format (bottom).</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p>
              Yet some compact laptops and some imported keyboards, built first for the American market or trimmed to save space, use an ANSI or hybrid chassis with AZERTY legends. The 105th key then physically does not exist, and &lt;&nbsp;&gt; become unreachable without a combination or software remapping. The system, for its part, still provides those characters: it is the physical keyboard that is amputated, not the layout.
            </p>
            <p>
              The phenomenon illustrates a distinction this whole page relies on: the <em>physical</em> format (ISO or ANSI), the key <em>marking</em> (what is engraved) and the <em>logical layout</em> (what the system produces) are three different layers, which do not always coincide. Much of the confusion about “the French keyboard” comes from there.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        {#- The stickers-and-keyboards box of the French pages (v2/encart-demandes.njk) is
            written in French inside the shared include, so its markup is repeated here in
            English, with the same classes, as on /en/. The request form exists in French
            only: the link goes to it and says so. `depuis` carries this page. -#}
        <aside class="encart-demandes carte" aria-labelledby="encart-demandes-titre">
          <div class="ac-touche ac-touche--noire" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ac-touche__face"></span><span class="ac-glyphe ac-glyphe--grave">O</span><span class="ac-autocollant"><span class="ac-glyphe">œ</span></span></div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="encart-demandes__titre" id="encart-demandes-titre">Stickers or an AZERTY&nbsp;Global keyboard?</h2>
