    {# VII. Research #}
    <section class="section" id="research">
      <div class="conteneur"><div class="mesure-lecture">
        <h2>Debates and research</h2>

        <details class="notice" id="studies">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span> Is AZERTY really badly suited to French? What does science say?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Two questions must be separated. AZERTY’s <em>gaps</em>, the impossibility of typing É, œ or « » under Windows, are documented and officially recognized: it is this finding that motivated the 2019 standard. On that ground, the debate is closed.
            </p>
            <p>
              But “badly suited” in the ergonomic sense (speed, fatigue, comfort) is another matter, and the research is surprisingly thin. The most serious work remains Yves Neuville’s thesis (University of Montpellier II, 1995), based on a field study of 117 professionals and comparative tests run from 1992 to 1995, work that the standard’s methodological document (<a href="https://inria.hal.science/hal-01826384" hreflang="fr">Nancel, 2018<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>) calls “of rare scope, certainly unique of its kind” (our translation). Since then? No modern comparative study, published in a peer-reviewed journal, on the contemporary AZERTY against its alternatives.
            </p>
            <p>
              The judgment “AZERTY is terrible”, everywhere online, therefore remains a massive feeling, widely shared and never measured. It is a scientific unknown, not an established truth: the nuance deserves defending on both sides of the debate.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="alternatives">
          <summary><span class="histoire-intitule"><span class="verdict verdict--conteste">Contested</span> Why didn’t bépo and Dvorak replace AZERTY?</span></summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              Two forces combine. First, the economics of change: relearning to type costs weeks of discomfort and slowness, and the installed base, teaching, shared workstations and compatibility all play for the status quo. A better layout is not enough: it has to be <em>enough</em> better to justify the toll, and for most users, it is not.
            </p>
            <p>
              Second, a little-known scientific debate: the paper “<a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=1069950">The Fable of the Keys</a>” (Liebowitz &amp; Margolis, 1990) re-examined the historical evidence for Dvorak’s superiority and showed that it came largely… from studies run by Dvorak himself. This does not prove that optimized layouts bring nothing, since the comfort reported by bépo or Ergo-L users is real and consistent, but no modern independent measurement quantifies it. Our <a href="/en/comparison">comparison</a> details what these layouts change, and for whom.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div></div>
    </section>

